  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "sB+banA52k/dLn30F2AwHqgsibQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:52.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## Two traps when verifying signatures\n\n**1. The Switchboard signs unsigned actions itself.** When an action arrives with no signature, the Switchboard signs it with its own `did:key`. The resulting operation therefore has a **non-empty `signatures` list** \u2014 which means the presence of signatures is *not* evidence that the client signed anything. A test or audit that asserts `signatures.length > 0` passes just as happily for a server-signed action as for a user-signed one.\n\nThe field to check is `context.signer.user.address`. That is what tells you *whose* identity is attached, and it is the only way to distinguish a user-attributed operation from a server-attributed one.\n\n**2. The server does not validate `prevOpHash`.** A wrong hash is accepted and the write succeeds. So conformance with the stamping contract cannot be inferred from a mutation returning successfully \u2014 it has to be asserted directly in tests, by reading the operation back and comparing `prevOpHash` against the hash you expect for that scope and revision.\n\n## Why this matters beyond tests\n\nTogether these two facts mean a write path can look fully signed and hash-chained while being neither. The failure is silent in both directions: the server fills in the signature you did not produce, and it accepts the hash you got wrong. Any claim that \"operations in this deployment are user-signed\" needs to be backed by checking the signer identity on real operations, not by the write path not erroring.\n\nThe practical check, on an operation read back from the Switchboard:\n\n```typescript\nconst signer = operation.action.context?.signer;\nconst wasClientSigned = Boolean(signer?.user?.address);\n```\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/08-ReactorBrowserClient/02-ApiReference.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:52.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:52.000Z"
    }
  },
